coder › Implemented `parseMoney` in `src/money.ts` and added `src/money.test.ts`.

What changed
- `parseMoney(text)` now returns `{ cents, currency }`.
- Currency detection supports `€`/`EUR`, `$`/`USD`, `£`/`GBP`; when several appear, the earliest one wins.
- Missing or unsupported currency throws a `TypeError` naming the text (e.g. `parseMoney: no supported currency in "..."`).
- Number parsing: a comma or dot followed by exactly two digits at the end of the number is the decimal separator; every other comma/dot/space is grouping.
- Leading `-` (including between the currency and the number, e.g. `€-1.234,56`) and parentheses around the amount make it negative.
- No digits throws a `TypeError` naming the text.

Decisions the plan didn't specify
- Currency codes are matched exactly: `USDT` does not count as `USD`, since `USD` must not be part of a longer letter run. Symbols match anywhere.
- Error messages follow the form `parseMoney: ... in "<text>"` so the text is always named.

